window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Quick start","h":"Quick start","u":"getting-started.html#quick-start","x":"Footsteps on your own character in about five minutes. No code."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"1. Open the Setup Wizard","u":"getting-started.html#1-open-the-setup-wizard","x":"Tools > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Setup Wizard. You can also right-click a character in the Hierarchy and choose All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Add Footsteps (Setup Wizard) , or right-click a prefab in the Project window."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"2. Pick your character","u":"getting-started.html#2-pick-your-character","x":"Drag it into Character , or select it and click Use selected . The wizard looks at it and tells you what it found: the controller (Character Controller, Rigidbody, Rigidbody 2D, NavMesh Agent or none) and where the feet are, whether it's the player (tagged Player or has a camera): the player's own steps play flat, the same in both ears, whether its animations already have footstep events, and its foot bones on a humanoid, whether another script already plays footsteps (Unity's Starter Assets do): the wizard can silence it. A prefab is never changed: the wizard saves a Prefab Variant beside it "},{"p":"Quick start","h":"3. Choose how it feels","u":"getting-started.html#3-choose-how-it-feels","x":"Pick a Preset (Realistic, Arcade, Stealth or Heavy) and where steps come from. Automatic works with every controller: a step every stride while the character moves on the ground."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"4. Map your surfaces","u":"getting-started.html#4-map-your-surfaces","x":"The wizard lists every material, terrain layer and tile in the open scene and what it plays now. It guesses from names (\"Grass_02\" is Grass, \"WoodenBridge\" is Wood). Change any guess, then click Apply . Using the sample library is fine to start; click Make my own copy before you change its sounds, so kit updates never overwrite your changes."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"5. Add Footsteps and play","u":"getting-started.html#5-add-footsteps-and-play","x":"Click Add Footsteps , then press Play and walk. The wizard checks the setup and lists anything that would stop steps from playing, each with a fix button. No sound, or the wrong one? Open Tools > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Why No Footstep? . It shows every step with its surface and why it was chosen, and the reason for any step it skipped."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"Setup Wizard","u":"setup-wizard.html#setup-wizard","x":"Tools > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Setup Wizard sets up footsteps on your own character and maps the surfaces in your scene. Nothing changes until you click Add Footsteps or Apply , and each is one undo step."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"1. Your character","u":"setup-wizard.html#1-your-character","x":"What it checks What it does with it Character Controller or Capsule Collider Puts the feet at the bottom of the capsule (the pivot is often in the middle). Rigidbody 2D Picks 2D side view when it has gravity, 2D top-down when it doesn't. No controller Steps follow the object's movement, whatever moves it. Feet go to the bottom of the model. Tagged Player, or has a camera Turns on Flat sound: the player's own steps sound the same in both ears. Humanoid Animator Finds the foot bones, so footprints land under each foot. Footstep animation events Recommends Animation Events for exact timing. Anoth"},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"2. How it feels","u":"setup-wizard.html#2-how-it-feels","x":"Preset: Realistic, Arcade, Stealth or Heavy. See Make it yours . Steps come from: Automatic, Animation Events, Foot Bones or Manual. See When steps happen . Flat sound (the player): on for the character you control, off for NPCs."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"3. Your surfaces","u":"setup-wizard.html#3-your-surfaces","x":"Pick a Surface Library . The sample one has 13 surfaces with sounds: Wood, Stone, Grass, Gravel, Dirt, Mud, Sand, Leaves, Metal, Water, Carpet, Snow and Ice. Make my own copy copies it and its surfaces into Assets/FootstepKitData , where kit updates never touch them. The table lists each material, terrain layer and tile in the open scene, how often it's used, and what it plays now (and why). The wizard's guess is already picked; change any, then click Apply to save them in the library as rules. Show selects an object that uses it."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"Done","u":"setup-wizard.html#done","x":"After Add Footsteps the wizard runs the setup check and lists any problem with a fix button, for example \"no Audio Listener in the scene\" or \"the ground is on a layer Ground Layers leaves out\"."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Demo scenes","u":"samples.html#demo-scenes","x":"Open them from Tools > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Open Demo . Controls: WASD or the left stick to move, Shift to run (hold longer to sprint), C or Ctrl to crouch, Space to jump. Click the game to look around with the mouse; Escape frees the mouse. M shows the Make it yours panel, P changes the feel, F turns footprints on and off, F4 shows the Why No Footstep? panel."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Showcase","u":"samples.html#showcase","x":"A walk from a wooden house (with a carpet) out to a stone patio and a garden: a terrain painted with grass, a gravel path, a snowy corner, a mud patch and fallen leaves. Cross the pond on the wooden bridge or wade through it, climb the metal stairs, then sneak past the guard in the gravel yard: crouch and it can't hear you, sprint and it turns to look. A knight NPC patrols with heavy steps and plate armour, and a robot patrols on its own metal feet whatever the ground (a Forced Surface); both are moved by a plain script that knows nothing about footsteps."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Feature Gallery","u":"samples.html#feature-gallery","x":"One pad of each of the 13 surfaces, a ledge to jump off for landings, a moving platform (standing still on it plays no steps), and a puddle that adds splashes on top of the stone. The Make it yours panel switches presets, footprints, effects, 3D or flat sound and volume while you play."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"2D Side View","u":"samples.html#2d-side-view","x":"A platformer level made of tiles: grass, wood, stone, metal, sand, mud, snow, ice, carpet and gravel. Each tile plays its own surface; the pool at the end splashes. The character uses Surface Footsteps set to 2D Side View. All demo content is generated, uses only what ships, and works in Built-in, URP and HDRP."},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","u":"surfaces.html#surfaces-and-the-surface-library","x":""},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Surfaces","u":"surfaces.html#surfaces","x":"A Surface (Assets > Create > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Surface) is one kind of ground: Wood, Grass, Metal, your own \"Squeaky Floorboards\". It holds: Setting What it does Display Name, Colour Shown in the debugger, gizmos and Scene Overview. Keywords Words that find this surface in material, texture, terrain layer, tile and object names (grass, lawn, turf). Walk, Run, Crouch, Jump, Land A sound set each. Run, Crouch and Land fall back to Walk when empty; Jump plays nothing when empty. Volume Volume of everything on this surface. Step Effect, Land Effect Optional prefabs (dust, splashes)"},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Your own sounds in one click","u":"surfaces.html#your-own-sounds-in-one-click","x":"Select your footstep clips (or a whole folder of them) in the Project window and choose Assets > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Make Surface From Selected Clips . It makes a new surface in Assets/FootstepKitData/Surfaces : Clips with run , sprint or jog in their name go to Run, crouch or sneak to Crouch, jump to Jump and land to Land; everything else is a walk step. The surface is named after the word the clip names share (\"Gravel_Step_01\", \"Gravel_Run_02\" makes \"Gravel\"), and that word becomes its keyword, so materials called \"Gravel...\" find it straight away. It's added to your own Surfac"},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Sounds included","u":"surfaces.html#sounds-included","x":"The sample library has 13 surfaces and more than 200 sounds, CC0 recordings plus a few of our own (see Third-Party Notices.txt): Wood, Stone, Grass, Gravel, Dirt, Mud, Sand, Leaves, Metal, Water, Carpet, Snow and Ice, with landing sounds for the soft surfaces, wood, metal and water. There are also gear sounds a character can add to every step (Leather, Cloth, Plate Armour, Armour Jingle) and a Robot Feet surface to force on a robot. Make your own copy (Setup Wizard) before changing them."},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"The Surface Library","u":"surfaces.html#the-surface-library","x":"A Surface Library (Create > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Surface Library) lists your surfaces and how to recognise them. Given what a foot touched, it checks in this order and stops at the first answer: A Footstep Zone the foot is inside (water, a rug). See Zones . A Footstep Surface Tag on the object or a parent: the surest way to say \"this is Metal\". Another kit's surface tag: any component with a public text property called SurfaceName that names a surface here (the All In One Prefab Builder Kit's Surface Tag works this way, no setup). A terrain layer under the foot (with Blend Two, th"},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Tips","u":"surfaces.html#tips","x":"Already have physics materials per ground? Map those once and every object using them is covered. Painted terrain at runtime? Call library.RefreshTerrain(terrainData) . Changed a material from code? Call SurfaceCacheVersion.Bump() ."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","u":"characters.html#surface-footsteps-your-characters","x":"Surface Footsteps goes on each character that should make steps: the player, NPCs, animals, remote players in a multiplayer game. It doesn't move the character and doesn't need to know how it moves."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"The main choices (top of the inspector)","u":"characters.html#the-main-choices-top-of-the-inspector","x":"Setting What it does Library Your surfaces. Profile How steps feel. Empty: the Preset below. New profile from preset makes an asset you can change and share. Steps come from Automatic, Animation Events, Foot Bones or Manual. See When steps happen . Dimension 3D, 2D Side View or 2D Top-Down. See 2D games . A status line under the title says \"Ready\" or the first problem, with Why No Footstep? and Setup Wizard buttons. In Play Mode it shows the last step and Test a step / Test a landing buttons play one on demand."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Ground check and feet","u":"characters.html#ground-check-and-feet","x":"Setting What it does Ground Layers Layers that count as ground. Leave on Everything unless the check hits things it shouldn't. Feet Offset Where the feet are, relative to the pivot. 0 when the pivot is at the feet. Check Height, Check Depth The ground check starts this far above the feet and reaches this far below. Raise the depth for stairs. Foot Spacing Steps and footprints alternate left and right of the centre. Left Foot, Right Foot Bones for the Foot Bones trigger and exact footprints. The wizard finds them on humanoids. Crouch Detection Collider Height (crouched when the capsule shrinks "},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"This character (overrides the profile)","u":"characters.html#this-character-overrides-the-profile","x":"Volume, pitch and stride scales; Footprints, Effects and Noise each Use Profile, On or Off; Spatial (Use Profile, Flat or Full 3D); Forced Surface (a robot always sounds like Metal); Surface Swaps (this giant's Wood sounds different); Gear Sounds played with every step (armour, chains, a squeaky shoe)."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Sound player and events","u":"characters.html#sound-player-and-events","x":"Sound Player: a component that implements IFootstepSoundPlayer plays the sounds instead (FMOD, Wwise, your own audio manager). See Extending . On Step, On Jump, On Land: UnityEvents for no-code wiring (camera bob, controller rumble)."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Swimming, ladders and teleports","u":"characters.html#swimming-ladders-and-teleports","x":"Pause Auto Steps / Resume Auto Steps (also callable from UnityEvents): no automatic steps, jumps or landings while swimming, climbing or in a cutscene. Your own Step() calls still play. ResetMotion() : call it after a teleport, respawn or reusing a pooled character. See Your controller and game for more."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Moving platforms, stairs, teleports","u":"characters.html#moving-platforms-stairs-teleports","x":"Standing still on a moving platform plays nothing: the kit takes the platform's movement out. Stairs work with the default check depth; raise Check Depth for tall steps. A jump of more than 4 m in one frame is never a step."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Your controller and game","u":"your-game.html#your-controller-and-game","x":"The kit doesn't move your character and doesn't need to know how it moves, so it fits most games as they are. This page covers the cases that need one extra setting or one line of code."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Which \"Steps come from\" to pick","u":"your-game.html#which-steps-come-from-to-pick","x":"Your character Pick Why First person, or any controller without foot animation Automatic A step every stride from the movement. Nothing to set up. Animated, and its clips already have footstep events (Starter Assets, many animation packs) Animation Events Steps land exactly when the foot does. Animated humanoid without events Foot Bones The kit watches each foot come down. Special movement with its own timing (a mech, a horse, network events) Manual Your code calls Step() , Jump() and Land() ."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Any character controller","u":"your-game.html#any-character-controller","x":"Put Surface Footsteps on the object that moves, or on the model under it. When it sits on the model and the controller is on a parent (a Character Controller, or a Rigidbody with a capsule), the kit treats the whole character as its own body: none of its colliders count as ground. Most controller packs (and Unity's Starter Assets) can play footsteps of their own. Turn those off , or every step is heard twice. The Setup Wizard silences Starter Assets for you, and Why No Footstep? warns when another script on the character looks like it plays footsteps. Walk, run and sprint come from the speed. "},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Swimming, ladders, sliding, riding, cutscenes","u":"your-game.html#swimming-ladders-sliding-riding-cutscenes","x":"Call Pause Auto Steps when the special movement starts and Resume Auto Steps when it ends. Both are in the UnityEvent list, so a trigger or your controller's events can call them without code: footsteps.SetAutoStepsPaused(true); // into the water, onto the ladder footsteps.Step(Foot.Left, FootstepMovement.Walk); // your own steps still play (a rung, a stroke) footsteps.SetAutoStepsPaused(false); // back on the ground: no stray landing"},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Teleports, respawns and pooled characters","u":"your-game.html#teleports-respawns-and-pooled-characters","x":"A jump of more than 4 m in one frame is never a step. For anything shorter (a respawn nearby, a pooled NPC reused), call footsteps.ResetMotion() right after moving it. Disabling and enabling the component resets it too."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Pause and slow motion","u":"your-game.html#pause-and-slow-motion","x":"With Time.timeScale at 0 nothing plays. Slow motion slows the steps with the movement, because steps follow distance. The kit's sounds are normal Audio Sources, so AudioListener.pause pauses them like the rest of your game's sound, and the profile's Audio Mixer Group sends them to your mixer."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Your own audio system (FMOD, Wwise, an audio manager)","u":"your-game.html#your-own-audio-system-fmod-wwise-an-audio-manager","x":"Put a component that implements IFootstepSoundPlayer in Sound Player . The kit still finds the surface, effects, footprints and AI noise, and hands each sound to your component instead of playing it. See Extending ."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Multiplayer","u":"your-game.html#multiplayer","x":"The kit has no network code and needs none. Pick one of these per character, not both: Every client plays steps from what it sees (simplest): put Surface Footsteps on every player and NPC with Automatic. Remote players' movement plays their steps on each client. Steps follow the owner's events: use Manual on remote copies and call Step() when your network event arrives. Give the local player Spatial: Flat and remote players Full 3D ."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Split screen","u":"your-game.html#split-screen","x":"Unity hears through one Audio Listener. With two players on one screen, use 3D sound with a generous Max Distance in the profile, or play local players' steps flat."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Many NPCs","u":"your-game.html#many-npcs","x":"Each character does one short ground check a frame and makes up to four Audio Sources when it first steps. For crowds: turn off Effects and Footprints on background NPCs (This character: Off), use a profile with a smaller Max Distance and a lower Priority for NPCs than for the player, disable Surface Footsteps on NPCs far from the camera, and enable it again when they come close."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Terrain","u":"your-game.html#terrain","x":"Terrain layers are read once, when the scene starts, in small pieces so even big terrains don't hitch. Several terrains side by side work: each step reads the terrain it hit, and holes are respected. Painted at runtime? Call library.RefreshTerrain(terrainData) . Loaded a terrain at runtime? Call library.PrewarmTerrains() . Terrain shaders that blend by vertex colour or inside the shader (some MicroSplat and Polybrush setups) can't be read from the terrain's layers. Use Footstep Zones for those areas, Footstep Surface Tags on objects, or map the terrain's layers that still exist."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Levels built at runtime and Addressables","u":"your-game.html#levels-built-at-runtime-and-addressables","x":"Nothing needs to be set up in advance: the first step on a new collider works out its surface, and the answer is remembered. If you change a material or tag from code, call SurfaceCacheVersion.Bump() ."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Ask the kit what a surface is","u":"your-game.html#ask-the-kit-what-a-surface-is","x":"Bullets, dropped items, tyres: use the same answer a footstep gets, zones included. if (library.TryResolveAt(position, out var result)) PlayImpact(result.surface); var fromHit = library.ResolveHit(hit);"},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"When steps happen","u":"triggers.html#when-steps-happen","x":"Steps come from on Surface Footsteps decides when a step plays."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Automatic (default)","u":"triggers.html#automatic-default","x":"A step every stride while the character moves on the ground. The stride is set per movement in the profile (walk 0.75 m, run 1.15 m, sprint 1.4 m, crouch 0.55 m in Realistic) and the movement comes from the speed (run from 3.5 m/s, sprint from 6.5 m/s). Works with every controller, NPCs moved by any script, and remote players, because it only watches movement. Jumps and landings are detected the same way: leaving the ground upwards faster than Jump Speed is a jump, touching down after Min Air Time is a landing, louder for a faster fall."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Animation Events","u":"triggers.html#animation-events","x":"For exact timing with animated characters. Add events to your walk and run clips with one of these function names: Footstep , FootstepLeft , FootstepRight , FootL , FootR , OnFootstep (the name Unity's Starter Assets and many animation packs already use), and OnLand for landings. Unity sends animation events to the Animator's own object. When Surface Footsteps sits on a parent (usual), add Footstep Animation Events to the Animator's object; the Setup Wizard and the debugger's fix button do it for you. In a blend tree or crossfade every clip fires its events. An event from a clip with half the "},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Foot Bones","u":"triggers.html#foot-bones","x":"For animated characters without events: the kit watches each foot bone and plays a step when it comes down. It learns how high each foot lifts, so it works with any rig and scale. Set Left Foot and Right Foot (the wizard finds them on humanoids)."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Manual","u":"triggers.html#manual","x":"Only when your code calls Step() , Step(Foot, FootstepMovement) , Jump() or Land(fallSpeed) . Use it for cutscenes, ladders or anything with its own timing."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Never machine-gun steps","u":"triggers.html#never-machine-gun-steps","x":"Min Step Interval (0.12 s in Realistic) is the shortest time between two steps, whatever triggers them. Skipped steps show \"Too soon\" in the debugger."},{"p":"Footstep Zones","h":"Footstep Zones","u":"zones.html#footstep-zones","x":"A Footstep Zone changes the steps inside an area: shallow water, a puddle, a rug on a wooden floor, a snowy patch on a road. Create one with GameObject > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Footstep Zone (a trigger box), or add the component to any object with a collider. The collider is the zone's shape; it can be a trigger. Setting What it does Surface The surface inside. Mode Replace : only the zone plays (wading in a pond, a rug). Add : the ground plays and the zone plays on top (a puddle on stone splashes and still sounds like stone). Priority When zones overlap, the highest wins. Added Vol"},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","u":"make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours-profiles-and-presets","x":"Everything a player hears, sees or that an AI reacts to can be changed or switched off, without code."},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Presets first","u":"make-it-yours.html#presets-first","x":"A Footstep Profile sets how steps feel. Start from a preset, then change any value: Preset Feel Realistic Natural strides, gentle variation, subtle dust and prints. Arcade Snappy and punchy: louder, bigger puffs, longer-lasting prints. Stealth Quiet: very soft crouching, and noise matters more to AI. Heavy Big, slow characters: long strides, low pitch, heavy landings. Create one with Create > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Footstep Profile or the New profile from preset button on Surface Footsteps. The profile inspector has a Preset picker and Apply preset at the top (one undo step). Withou"},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Everything in a profile","u":"make-it-yours.html#everything-in-a-profile","x":"Group Settings Volume and pitch Volume, pitch, pitch variation, walk / run / sprint / crouch volume. Stride Walk, run, sprint and crouch stride; run and sprint speeds; minimum speed; min step interval. Jump and landing Play jump, play land, jump speed, min air time, soft and hard landing speeds, landing volume. Effects Show effects, effect scale. Footprints Show footprints, footprint picture, material, size, lifetime, fade, maximum at once. 3D sound Spatial blend, min and max distance, rolloff, Audio Mixer group, priority. Noise for AI Emit noise; how far walk, run, sprint, crouch and landing "},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Per character","u":"make-it-yours.html#per-character","x":"Each Surface Footsteps can override its profile: volume, pitch and stride scales, footprints / effects / noise on or off, flat or 3D sound, a forced surface, surface swaps and gear sounds. See Surface Footsteps ."},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Your own sounds, pictures and audio system","u":"make-it-yours.html#your-own-sounds-pictures-and-audio-system","x":"Every sound is a clip you choose, per surface and movement. Every effect is your own prefab; footprints use your own picture (a right foot pointing up; the left is mirrored). Bring your own audio: put a component implementing IFootstepSoundPlayer in Sound Player and the kit hands it every step (FMOD, Wwise, your manager). See Extending . Switch anything off: no effects, no footprints, no AI noise, no jump sound, or mute a surface. The demos' Make it yours panel (M) switches presets, footprints, effects, flat or 3D sound and volume while you play."},{"p":"Effects and footprints","h":"Effects and footprints","u":"effects-and-footprints.html#effects-and-footprints","x":""},{"p":"Effects and footprints","h":"Step effects","u":"effects-and-footprints.html#step-effects","x":"Give a surface a Step Effect prefab (and optionally a Land Effect ): dust on gravel, splashes in water, bits of grass. Any prefab works; particle systems in it are restarted each time. Effects are pooled (up to 16 copies of each), so steps never create objects or garbage. Landings scale the effect up for a harder fall. The profile's Effect Scale sizes them all; Show Effects turns them off. The samples include Dust Puff, Grass Bits, Splash and Snow Puff, drawn with the kit's own shader."},{"p":"Effects and footprints","h":"Footprints","u":"effects-and-footprints.html#footprints","x":"Footprints appear on surfaces with Footprints ticked (Snow, Gravel and Grass in the sample library), in that surface's Footprint Colour , when the profile's Show Footprints is on and it has a Footprint Picture . They're pooled ( Max Footprints , oldest reused), stay for Lifetime seconds and fade over Fade seconds. Each print follows the ground's slope and the direction of travel; left and right alternate (the left is the picture mirrored). With foot bones set, prints land exactly under each foot. Landings leave both feet. Footprints use the kit's Unlit Transparent shader, which has Built-in, U"},{"p":"AI hearing (stealth)","h":"AI hearing (stealth)","u":"ai-hearing.html#ai-hearing-stealth","x":"Every step can be heard by AI. How far it carries is the profile's noise distance for the movement (walk 6 m, run 10 m, sprint 14 m, crouch 1.5 m, landing 12 m in Realistic) times the surface's Loudness (soft grass 0.6, metal 1.6) times the character's volume. Crouching on grass is nearly silent; sprinting on metal carries far."},{"p":"AI hearing (stealth)","h":"Footstep Listener","u":"ai-hearing.html#footstep-listener","x":"Add Footstep Listener to a guard: Setting What it does Hearing 1 normal, 2 hears twice as far, 0 deaf. Walls Muffle A step behind something solid carries only half as far. Wall Layers What counts as a wall. Ignore Own Steps The guard doesn't hear its own feet. On Heard A UnityEvent with the step's position: turn, investigate, raise the alarm. From code, Heard gives the whole step (who, where, which surface, how loud), and LastHeardPosition , LastHeardTime and LastHeardDistance01 are always available. Your own AI can listen to every step without a component: FootstepNoise.Made += step => ... . "},{"p":"2D games","h":"2D games","u":"2d.html#2d-games","x":"Set Dimension on Surface Footsteps (the Setup Wizard picks it from a Rigidbody 2D)."},{"p":"2D games","h":"2D Side View (platformers)","u":"2d.html#2d-side-view-platformers","x":"The ground check is a 2D ray down from the feet. Tilemaps with a Tilemap Collider 2D play each tile's surface: map tiles (or their sprites) to surfaces in the wizard or Scene Overview, or name them with a keyword (\"GrassTop\"). Other 2D colliders work like 3D ones: Surface Tags, physics materials, sprite materials and names. Zones use 2D colliders. Footprints are off in side view (there's no floor to print on)."},{"p":"2D games","h":"2D Top-Down","u":"2d.html#2d-top-down","x":"The floor is whatever is under the character's position: 2D colliders there (triggers too, for floor areas), then the Floor Tilemaps you list (top layer last), then the default surface. Footprints lie flat facing the camera and point the way the character moves."},{"p":"2D games","h":"Sound","u":"2d.html#sound","x":"2D games usually want flat sound: set Spatial to Flat on the player, or Spatial Blend 0 in the profile. The 2D Side View demo walks across grass, wood, stone, metal, snow, carpet and gravel tiles into a pool."},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","u":"why-no-footstep.html#why-no-footstep-and-scene-overview","x":""},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"Why No Footstep? (window)","u":"why-no-footstep.html#why-no-footstep-window","x":"Tools > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Why No Footstep? Pick a character (or select it in the Hierarchy). Setup check lists every problem it finds, most serious first, each with a fix button where one exists: Problem One-click fix No Surface Library Use the sample library (or yours) The library has no default surface Set a default A surface has no Walk sounds Select the surface The ground is on a layer Ground Layers leaves out (\"Floor is on layer Environment\") Include that layer The feet are below or above the ground (\"feet are 0.90 m below the ground\") Put the feet on the ground The ground"},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"In-game panel","u":"why-no-footstep.html#in-game-panel","x":"In-game panel adds a Footstep Debug Overlay to the scene: the same live view inside the Game view and in development builds. F4 shows and hides it (change the key, the corner and the rows in its inspector). With Development Only on (the default) it never shows in a release build."},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"Scene View","u":"why-no-footstep.html#scene-view","x":"Select a character to see its ground check (blue) and the last steps: a line and dot per step in the surface's colour, red for a step that didn't play."},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"Scene Overview","u":"why-no-footstep.html#scene-overview","x":"Tools > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Scene Overview shows the whole scene: Characters: each one with its library, profile and any problems (with fixes). Surfaces: every material, terrain layer and tile, what it plays now and why, with a picker to map it. Zones: every zone, its surface and mode; adds a shape to a zone without one."},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Extending with code","u":"extending.html#extending-with-code","x":"Everything here is optional: the kit needs no code. Namespace RomanoAssets.FootstepKit ."},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Listen to steps","u":"extending.html#listen-to-steps","x":"var footsteps = GetComponent<SurfaceFootsteps>(); footsteps.Stepped += step => { // step.surface.surface: the FootstepSurface; step.surface.source: why it was chosen // step.point, step.normal, step.foot, step.movement (Walk, Run, Sprint, Crouch, Jump, Land) // step.volume, step.intensity (landings: how hard), step.noiseRadius, step.addedZone }; footsteps.Attempted += record => { /* every attempt, with record.result when it didn't play */ };"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Play steps yourself","u":"extending.html#play-steps-yourself","x":"footsteps.Trigger = FootstepTrigger.Manual; footsteps.Step(); // the current movement, alternating feet footsteps.Step(Foot.Left, FootstepMovement.Run); footsteps.Jump(); footsteps.Land(fallSpeed: 7f); footsteps.SetCrouching(true); footsteps.SetAutoStepsPaused(true); // swimming, ladders, cutscenes (Step() still works) footsteps.ResetMotion(); // after a teleport or respawn"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Your own audio system","u":"extending.html#your-own-audio-system","x":"public sealed class FmodFootsteps : MonoBehaviour, IFootstepSoundPlayer { public void PlayFootstep(in FootstepEvent step, AudioClip clip, float volume, float pitch) { // Play your event with step.surface.surface.DisplayName as a parameter, at step.point. } } Drag the component into Sound Player on Surface Footsteps. The kit then plays nothing itself."},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Ask what a surface is","u":"extending.html#ask-what-a-surface-is","x":"SurfaceResolution r = library.ResolveHit(hit); // zones first, then the collider, like a footstep if (library.TryResolveAt(position, out var below)) { } // cast down from a point, no character needed if (r.Found) Debug.Log(r.surface.DisplayName + \" \" + FootstepDiagnostics.SourceWords(r)); library.SetRule(myMaterial, metalSurface); // map from code library.RefreshTerrain(terrain.terrainData); // after painting at runtime SurfaceCacheVersion.Bump(); // after changing materials or tags from code library.PrewarmTerrains(); // after loading a terrain at runtime (no hitch on the first step)"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"AI hearing","u":"extending.html#ai-hearing","x":"FootstepNoise.Made += step => { if (step.noiseRadius > Vector3.Distance(step.point, transform.position)) Investigate(step.point); };"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Setup checks from code","u":"extending.html#setup-checks-from-code","x":"var problems = new List<FootstepProblem>(); FootstepDiagnostics.Check(footsteps, problems); // the same checks as the debugger window The kit has no static state that survives Play Mode, so it works with Domain Reload disabled."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"upgrading.html#upgrading","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Updating the kit","u":"upgrading.html#updating-the-kit","x":"Back up your project (or commit it). Import the new version from the Package Manager (My Assets). Read the changelog section for your version. Your own content lives outside the kit's folder ( Assets/FootstepKitData for libraries, surfaces and profiles made by the tools), so an update never overwrites it. If you changed the sample library or surfaces directly, make your own copy first (Setup Wizard > Make my own copy): updates replace the sample files. The kit's folder can be moved or renamed in your project; tools find their files wherever they are."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Coming from another footstep tool","u":"upgrading.html#coming-from-another-footstep-tool","x":"Your existing physics materials or tags per ground can be mapped once in the Surface Library. Animation events named OnFootstep (Starter Assets and many packs) already work with Animation Events. Remove or silence the other tool's audio so you don't hear both (the wizard offers this for Starter Assets)."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Version history","u":"upgrading.html#version-history","x":"See CHANGELOG.md in the kit's folder."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting","x":"Start with Tools > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Why No Footstep? : it checks the setup and explains every step. What you see Why, and what to do No steps at all Is there an Audio Listener in the scene? Is the character moving faster than Min Speed (0.25 m/s)? The window's Live view shows both. \"No ground\" in the debugger The ground check misses the ground: the feet are in the wrong place (Feet Offset), the check is too short (Check Depth), or the ground's layer isn't in Ground Layers. The setup check finds which and fixes it. The wrong surface plays The debugger says why each surface was "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Known issues","u":"troubleshooting.html#known-issues","x":"None yet. Found one? See Support ."},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"support.html#support","x":"Need help? Every message gets an answer. Documentation: https://romanoassets.github.io/footstep-kit/docs/ Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu (ask in #help) Report a bug: https://github.com/romanoassets/RomanoAssetSupport/issues Website: https://romanoassets.github.io/footstep-kit/ Email: marklromano@outlook.com In Unity: Tools > All In One Surface Footstep Kit > Help and Support , then Copy Support Info (kit version, Unity version, render pipeline and platform) and paste it in your message. A screenshot of the Why No Footstep? window helps too. Enjoying the kit? A short review on the Asset"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":"All notable changes to the All In One Surface Footstep Kit. The format follows Keep a Changelog; versions follow Semantic Versioning."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.1] - 2026-11-08","u":"changelog.html#101---2026-11-08","x":"Smoother fitting into your own game. Nothing to do when you update: your settings and characters keep working."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added","x":"Pause Auto Steps / Resume Auto Steps (and SetAutoStepsPaused ): no automatic steps while swimming, climbing a ladder, sliding or in a cutscene; your own Step() calls still play, and resuming never plays a stray landing. ResetMotion() : call it after a teleport, respawn or when a pooled character is reused, so the move is never a step. Surface queries without a character: library.TryResolveAt(position, out result) and library.ResolveHit(hit) , the same answer a footstep gets (zones included), for bullet impacts, dropped items or tyres. Min Event Weight (Animation Events): in blend trees and cro"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Fixed","u":"changelog.html#fixed","x":"Surface Footsteps on a child model now ignores every collider of the character above it (a Character Controller, or a Rigidbody with a capsule), not only the model's own. Big terrains no longer hitch or allocate a large temporary array on the first step: layers are read in small bands when the scene starts ( library.PrewarmTerrains() after loading a terrain at runtime)."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0] - 2026-11-07","u":"changelog.html#100---2026-11-07","x":"First release."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added_1","x":"Surface Footsteps: footsteps for any character (player, NPC, remote player, 2D), from movement, animation events or foot bones, with jumps, landings, crouching, running and sprinting. Surfaces and the Surface Library: sounds, effects, footprints and AI loudness per surface, found from Surface Tags, terrain layers, tiles, physics materials, materials, textures, names and Unity tags, with a default surface. Footstep Zones for water, puddles and rugs (replace the ground, or add on top). Footstep Profiles with four presets (Realistic, Arcade, Stealth, Heavy) and per-character overrides. Why No Foo"}];
